        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          STS-93 was the 26th flight of the Space Shuttle Columbia and the 94th Shuttle mission.  Columbias five member crew included Commander Eileen M. Collins, Pilot Jeffrey S. Ashby, and Mission Specialists Steve A. Hawley, Catherine G. Coleman, and Michel Tognini.  Astronaut Collins was the first American woman assigned to command a Space Shuttle; she was also the first woman to pilot a Space Shuttle, serving that function on STS-63 and STS-84.  After two launch postponements caused by a computer malfunction and unacceptable weather conditions, the Space Shuttle Columbia lifted off from Kennedy Space Center (KSC) on July 23, 1999.<P>The primary objective during the STS-93 mission was the deployment of the Advanced X-Ray Astrophysics Facility (AXAF), the most sophisticated x-ray observatory ever built.  The facility was renamed the Chandra X-Ray Observatory in honor of the late Indian-American Nobel Laureate Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar.  "Chandra" also means moon in Sanskrit.  The observatory is composed of three major parts: (1) an x-ray telescope with mirrors that focus on x-rays from celestial objects; (2) science instruments to record the x-rays so that images can be produced and analyzed; and (3) the spacecraft, which provided the environment necessary for the telescope and instruments to work.  The Chandra X-ray Observatory was designed to observe x-rays from high-energy regions of the universe, such as hot gas in the remnants of exploded stars.<P>Life sciences payloads on STS-93 included the Plant Growth Investigations in Microgravity (PGIM-01), Biological Research in a Canister-11 (BRIC-11), BRIC-12/Space Tissue Loss-B (BRIC-12/STL-B) and National Institutes of Health-B1 (NIH.B1a) experiment.  PGIM-01 used plants to monitor stressful conditions in the space flight environment that affect plant growth.  Because plants move away from stressful conditions, investigators have developed mechanisms to monitor the environment and direct effective physiological responses to harmful conditions.   <P>BRIC payloads consist of passive experiments contained within a metal canister that is exposed to the temperatures, carbon dioxide, oxygen, atmospheric pressure and humidity conditions of the Shuttle middeck.  The BRIC-11 payload consisted of experiments designed to investigate the effects of space flight on small arthropod animals and plants.  The BRIC-12/STL-B payload studied the early development of fern gametophytes in microgravity.<P>NIH.B1a investigated the effects of space flight on nervous system development and neuromuscular synapse formation in the embryos and larvae of <I>Drosophila melanogaster</I>, more commonly known as the fruit fly.<P>Other payloads on this mission included the Midcourse Space Experiment, Shuttle Ionospheric Modification with Pulsed Local Exhaust, Southwest Ultraviolet Imaging System, Gelation of Sols: Applied Microgravity Research, Light Weight Flexible Solar Array Hinge, Cell Culture Module, and the Shuttle Amateur Radio Experiment-II, EarthKam, Commercial Generic Bioprocessing Apparatus, and the Micro-Electrical Mechanical System.<P>The Space Shuttle Columbia touched down at Kennedy Space Center on July 27, 1999.  This was the 12th night landing in the Shuttle program's history.<END>
